`List.IndexOf` already performs the left-to-right search needed here. It returns the first matching index, or `-1` when the value is absent, so the method can simply pass the result back.

This also covers an empty list without a separate condition. The list is not changed by the lookup.

```csharp
using System.Collections.Generic;

public class Solution {
    public int LinearSearch(List<int> nums, int target) {
        return nums.IndexOf(target);
    }
}
```

`IndexOf` takes `O(n)` time in the worst case and `O(1)` extra space.

A plain `for` loop keeps this implementation small. It visits the slice in order, so duplicates are handled correctly by returning the first match. No extra collection is needed.

For `[10, 3, 8, 3]` with target `3`, the function returns `1` because that is the first place where the target appears.

```go
func linearSearch(nums []int, target int) int {
    for index, value := range nums {
        if value == target {
            return index
        }
    }
    return -1
}
```

The running time is `O(n)` in the worst case, with `O(1)` extra space. The slice contents are only read.

This version checks one position at a time through a small recursive helper. When the current position reaches the end, there is no match and `-1` is returned. Otherwise, a matching value gives its index immediately.

An empty array also works because the first call sees that the index is already outside the array.

```java
class Solution {
    public int linearSearch(int nums[], int target) {
        return searchFrom(nums, target, 0);
    }

    private int searchFrom(int[] nums, int target, int index) {
        if (index == nums.length) {
            return -1;
        }
        if (nums[index] == target) {
            return index;
        }
        return searchFrom(nums, target, index + 1);
    }
}
```

The scan takes `O(n)` time. The recursive calls use `O(n)` stack space in the worst case. The array stays unchanged.

A direct scan is enough here. The loop checks each value from left to right and returns the first index where it matches `target`. If the loop ends, the target is not present, so the method returns `-1`.

For example, with `nums = [4, 9, 2, 9]` and `target = 9`, the scan stops at index `1`, not index `3`.

```cpp
#include <vector>
using namespace std;

class Solution {
public:
    int linearSearch(vector<int>& nums, int target) {
        for (int i = 0; i < static_cast<int>(nums.size()); ++i) {
            if (nums[i] == target) {
                return i;
            }
        }
        return -1;
    }
};
```

Time complexity: `O(n)` in the worst case.
Space complexity: `O(1)`. The input array is not changed.

